From 16e9249b379a72acbabef9ae182d0287b28c0cbe Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Marco <mpalinar@nmblr.ai>
Date: Fri, 21 Aug 2026 15:38:23 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 10/12] Commit each streamed matrix's upload immediately
 instead of batching four

A block's four matrices shared one command buffer committed only after all four
preads finished, so no DMA started until the last byte reached staging.
Committing each copy as it is encoded, and waiting for all of them at the end of
the block, lets the transfer of matrix i overlap the pread of matrix i+1. This
also removes the shared blit encoder, so the code is simpler than before.

Measured at 608x352 / 22 frames / 4 steps / 50 layers:

  host->VRAM DMA wait      92.0s -> 63.3s   (-31%)
  stream total            124.6s -> 96.0s   (-23%, 1.158 -> 1.502 GiB/s)
  unhidden stream wait      2.5s ->  0.025s
  DiT Euler denoise wall  139.0s -> 141.0s  (unchanged)
  total run               242.7s -> 243.9s  (unchanged, within noise)

So the transfer path is meaningfully faster and the end-to-end time is not.
Something else now bounds the denoise: the phase reports wait=140.7s against
10.1s of GPU compute and a fully hidden stream, which the current counters do
not explain. Committed anyway because the DMA critical path is shorter, the
code is simpler, and output stays bit-identical -- but the projected end-to-end
win did not materialise and the next estimate should not assume it.
